   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the New York city civil court act, in relation to the appointment of members of the advisory council for the housing part",
    "basePrintNo": "S7621",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7621-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §110, NYC Civ Ct Act",
    "lawSection": "New York City Civil Court Act",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7621\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the New York city civil court act, in relation to the\nappointment of members of the advisory council for the housing part\n\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS OF BILL :\nSection 1 of the bill would amend subdivision (g) of section 110 of\nthe New York City Civil Court Act to provide for the addition of one\ntenant member to the advisory council and for the addition of the\nchair of the New York City Housing Authority as an additional real\nestate member on the council. The two new members added by this bill\nwould replace two of the four public at large members of the advisory\ncouncil, thus maintaining the advisory committee at fourteen members.\n\nSection 2 provides that the bill is effective January 1, 2011.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nUnder section 110 (g) of the New York City Civil Court Act, the\nAdministrative Judge selects housing judges from a list of persons\nfound qualified by the advisory council. The selection of housing\ncourt judges is subject to the approval of the Appellate Divisions of\nthe First and Second Judicial Departments. The advisory council\ncurrently consists of two members each of the following groups: real\nestate industry, tenants' organizations, civic groups and bar\nassociations. In addition the advisory council contains four members\nof the public at large, a representative of the Mayor (HPD) and a\nrepresentative of the Commissioner of Housing and Community Renewal\n(HCR). The public interest in the administration of justice is best\nserved where the advisory council represents the major participants in\nthe process.\n\nAn additional tenant representative will add needed balance to the\nadvisory council. The NYCHA member is important because NYCHA provides\nhousing for over 600,000 residents in the five boroughs.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect January 1, 2011.\n",
